<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Montserrat</h1><p class="st-meta">1632 CE – present · capital Plymouth; later Brades</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small volcanic island in the Leewards, settled by Irish Catholics in the 17th century and still a British Overseas Territory, partly buried by its volcano.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>English and Irish settlers, many of them Catholics from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Saint Kitts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, were sent to Montserrat in 1632 under Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-warner">Thomas Warner</a>. The island soon had an unusually large Irish population, and later arrivals included Irish prisoners transported after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Cromwell</a>'s campaigns. The French captured it twice, in 1667 and 1782, but each time it returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> or <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governed by a governor with an assembly and council, Montserrat grew tobacco, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and limes on estates worked by enslaved Africans. The Irish legacy survives in place names, surnames and a public holiday on Saint Patrick's Day, which also marks a slave uprising planned for 17 March 1768. From 1871 to 1956 it formed part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/leeward-islands-colony">Leeward Islands colony</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Montserrat chose to remain a British dependency when its neighbours became independent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soufriere-hills">Soufrière Hills volcano</a> erupted from 1995, destroying the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plymouth-montserrat">Plymouth</a> in 1997 and forcing two thirds of the people to leave. It continues as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territory</a> with a new administrative centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brades">Brades</a> in the north.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/montserrat.jpg" alt="Montserrat" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Montserrat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1632 CE – present</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Plymouth; later Brades</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Anthony Briskett</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism and Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Montserrat</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Montserrat</dd></dl></aside></div>

